A clean, precise technical DRAFTING-BOARD CONSTRUCTION on off-white drafting paper, straight-on. A solid horizontal STRIP runs left to right with its ends labelled A (left) and B (right). The task, titled 'DIVIDE THE STRIP A B INTO FIVE EQUAL PARTS', is to divide the strip into FIVE parts of EXACTLY equal length using the intercept-theorem ruler-and-compass construction:
  - from A, draw an auxiliary straight ray going up and to the right at a shallow angle ABOVE the strip;
  - with a compass set to a fixed opening, step off FIVE equal steps along that ray, showing the faint compass arcs at each step;
  - join the fifth step point to B, then draw lines parallel to it through the other four step points; where those parallels meet the strip they mark the division lines.

The result is FOUR interior division lines drawn ACROSS the strip that split it into five identical parts -- they must fall at exactly one fifth, two fifths, three fifths and four fifths of the length from A to B. Draw the strip as a solid bar with four crisp full-height interior division lines at the exact fifths, and show the auxiliary ray with its five equal compass steps and the parallels above the strip. Do NOT space the interior lines by eye -- they must be the exact fifths produced by the construction. No numbers, no measurements, no text other than the A and B labels and the title.
